{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small retail shop in Nairobi with 3 employees. I've been keeping basic sales records in a notebook. A friend told me I should get proper accounting done. Based on what you know about Kenya's accountancy regulation, do I need to hire a qualified accountant or get my records audited?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The source text I have is from the Institute of Certified Public Accountants of Kenya (ICPAK), which is the body that develops and regulates the accountancy profession in Kenya to protect the public interest. However, the specific text provided doesn't detail which business sizes or types are legally required to use certified accountants or undergo audits. To give you a proper answer about whether your 3-person retail shop needs formal accounting or auditing, I'd need to check Kenya's tax laws and Companies Act requirements for your business structure and turnover. What I can tell you is that ICPAK is the official regulator, so if you do decide to hire an accountant, you should verify they are ICPAK-certified. I'd recommend contacting ICPAK directly at +254 719 074 000 or memberservices@icpak.com to confirm the requirements for your specific business."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "icpak_about_2026.txt", "source_text": "Menu To develop, regulate and promote an internationally recognized accountancy profession that upholds public interest through capacity building, advocacy, innovation and sustainable practices. Contact Information P.O BOX 59963-00200 CPA Center, Ruaraka, Thika road. Nairobi, Kenya. Telephone Line: +254 719 074 000 Email: memberservices@icpak.com Facebook Linkedin Youtube Whatsapp Member Of Crafted with Brait Consulting Limited Quick Links Lodge a Complaint Fire Awards FAQs Portal Login Find A C"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm running a small savings group in my community and we're thinking about becoming a formal microfinance institution. What does the law say we need to know about regulatory requirements?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "According to Kenyan law, microfinance institutions are regulated under the Microfinance Act 2006 and the Microfinance (Deposit Taking Institutions) Regulations 2008. These laws set out the legal, regulatory and supervisory framework that microfinance businesses must follow. The regulations are designed to ensure that microfinance institutions maintain a sound financial position and conduct their business properly, so that the overall financial system stays stable and protected. If you're planning to take deposits from members, you'll need to understand and comply with this regulatory framework. I'd recommend speaking with the Central Bank of Kenya to get clear guidance on what specific requirements apply to your group as it transitions to a formal institution."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "d mortgage finance companies are subject to regulatory requirements governing their prudential position and market conduct in order to safeguard the overall soundness and stability of the financial system. Commercial Banks and Mortgage Finance Companies https://www.centralbank.go.ke/contact-us/ The Microfinance Act 2006 and the Microfinance (Deposit Taking Institutions) Regulations 2008 set out the legal, regulatory and supervisory framework for the microfinance industry in Kenya. The Microfinan"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small savings group with 15 members in Nairobi. We collect money from members monthly and give loans to members who need them. Do I need to follow the Microfinance Act 2006 rules, or am I exempt?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Based on the Microfinance Act 2006 and the Microfinance (Deposit Taking Institutions) Regulations 2008, if your savings group is taking deposits from members and giving out loans, you may fall under microfinance regulations. The key question is whether you are formally registered as a deposit-taking microfinance institution. A casual savings group that only pools money from its own members for internal lending may operate differently than a registered microfinance institution. However, if you are collecting deposits and operating like a financial service, you should check with the Central Bank of Kenya to confirm whether your group needs to be licensed and comply with the prudential and market conduct requirements that microfinance institutions must follow. It is safer to contact the CBK directly rather than assume you are exempt."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "d mortgage finance companies are subject to regulatory requirements governing their prudential position and market conduct in order to safeguard the overall soundness and stability of the financial system. Commercial Banks and Mortgage Finance Companies https://www.centralbank.go.ke/contact-us/ The Microfinance Act 2006 and the Microfinance (Deposit Taking Institutions) Regulations 2008 set out the legal, regulatory and supervisory framework for the microfinance industry in Kenya. The Microfinan"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm running a small retail shop as a sole proprietor, and I've been thinking about formalizing it. A friend suggested I could register it as a company limited by guarantee instead. My shop has 2 employees and makes about 500,000 shillings per year. What would change about my personal liability if I did this?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "If you register as a company limited by guarantee, your personal liability would be limited to whatever amount you agree to contribute to the company's assets. Right now, as a sole proprietor, you're personally responsible for all debts and obligations of the business – meaning creditors can come after your personal assets. With a limited guarantee company, if the business owes money or faces a lawsuit, your personal risk is capped at the amount you promised to put in. So if you agree to guarantee 100,000 shillings, that's your maximum personal exposure. This protects your personal property from business problems. However, note that a company limited by guarantee doesn't have share capital, so it works differently from other company structures – it's more commonly used for non-profit or community organizations, not typical retail shops. For a small retail business like yours, you might want to explore other options like a private company limited by shares, which also limits liability but is more standard for commercial operations."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "o limit on the liability of its members Public limited company A public company has share capital and limits the liability of each member to the amount unpaid on their shares. It may offer its shares for sale to the general public and may be listed at the securities Company limited by guarantee This type of company does not have share capital and its members are guarantors rather than shareholders. The liability of members is limited to the amount they agree to contribute to the company’s assets"}}
