{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm running a small retail shop in Nairobi as a sole proprietor right now, but I'm thinking about bringing in my brother as a co-owner. Do I need to incorporate as a company under the Companies Act, or can we continue operating as we are?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Based on the Companies Act, 2015, you have a choice. Right now, as a sole proprietor, you're not incorporated—you're operating as an individual. However, if you want to bring in your brother as a co-owner, you can incorporate and register a company at the Companies Registry. The good news is that the law allows one or more legal persons—including individuals like you and your brother—to form a company together by subscribing to a memorandum of association. So yes, you can formalize your partnership by incorporating, but it's not mandatory if you both want to stay informal. If you do decide to incorporate, you'll have different company types to choose from, each with different structures. You should check which type of company makes sense for your retail business before you apply to the Companies Registry."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "been incorporated and registered at the Companies Registry under the Companies Act, 2015. Who can incorporate a company? One or more legal persons (which includes individuals, companies, government-owned entities, non-profit organizations and other legal persons excluding an unincorporated Trust) can form a company by subscribing their names to a memorandum of association. Which types of companies are available? There are five types of companies: Company Type Details Private company limited by s"}}
