{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm running a small flour milling business in Kenya. What do I need to know about food fortification requirements for my products?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Food fortification means adding vitamins and minerals to food to prevent health deficiencies—the government says it's an effective health strategy. In Kenya, the Ministry of Public Health made it mandatory under Legal Notice 62 from June 2012. If you're milling wheat flour, you must fortify it with Zinc and Iron. This is a legal requirement, not optional. You should check with your local health authorities or the Ministry to understand exactly how to fortify your flour and what documentation you need to keep to show compliance."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "ood Fortification Mark Food fortification is the addition of one or more vitamin and/or mineral to a food to correct or prevent a demonstrated micronutrient deficiency. It is one of the four key cost effective strategies recommended by WHO in curbing micronutrient deficiency. The Ministry of Public Health and Sanitation through a Legal Notice no. 62 of 15th June 2012 has therefore declared it mandatory to fortify the following food products; 1-Wheat Flour – with Zinc & Iron 2-Dry Milled Maize Pr"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm starting a small flour milling business in Kenya and plan to sell wheat flour. Create a simple checklist of what I need to do to make sure my wheat flour meets the government's fortification requirements."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's what you need to do for your wheat flour to comply with Kenyan law:\n\n**Wheat Flour Fortification Checklist**\n\n1. **Know the requirement**: Kenya's Ministry of Public Health and Sanitation made it mandatory (Legal Notice no. 62 of 15th June 2012) to fortify all wheat flour you produce or sell.\n\n2. **Add the required nutrients**: Your wheat flour must be fortified with:\n   - Zinc\n   - Iron\n\n3. **Check your supply chain**: Make sure your suppliers or your milling process includes these fortification steps before the flour reaches customers.\n\n4. **Keep records**: Document that fortification is happening at your production stage.\n\n5. **Label correctly**: Ensure your packaging clearly states that the flour is fortified (this helps you comply and informs your customers).\n\nThis fortification is not optional—it's the law. The government requires it as a public health measure to prevent micronutrient deficiencies in the population."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "drafting", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "ood Fortification Mark Food fortification is the addition of one or more vitamin and/or mineral to a food to correct or prevent a demonstrated micronutrient deficiency. It is one of the four key cost effective strategies recommended by WHO in curbing micronutrient deficiency. The Ministry of Public Health and Sanitation through a Legal Notice no. 62 of 15th June 2012 has therefore declared it mandatory to fortify the following food products; 1-Wheat Flour – with Zinc & Iron 2-Dry Milled Maize Pr"}}
