{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small farm in Kenya and I'm trying to understand the role of women in agriculture and water management. What does the regulatory and development literature say about this?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "According to the source material, women play a very significant role in Kenya's agricultural sector—they make up about 75 percent of the agricultural labour force. Beyond farming, in many Kenyan communities, women also carry the responsibility of fetching water for household use. This work becomes especially difficult during drought periods, when women have to spend many hours and travel long distances just to find water. This also makes women and communities relying on low-lying riverine areas and floodplains particularly vulnerable to the effects of climate change and changing rainfall patterns. So if you're running a farm, it's important to recognize that women workers and community members depend on reliable water access, and climate-related changes can significantly impact their ability to work and support their households."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb7_culture_context", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "kenya_unfccc_adaptation_report_2025.pdf", "source_text": "informal they play significant roles.45 For example, women comprise settlements are most at risk and vulnerable to the adverse 75 percent of the agricultural labour force in Kenya.46 Across effects of climate change as they largely occupy low lying many communities, women are responsible for fetching water riverine areas and floodplains. for household use. During drought, they spend many hours The expected changes in rainfall, coupled with increased and trek long distances to obtain water for d"}}
