{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm running a small training center in Nairobi and want to understand what the key issues are around instructor capability in TVET institutions. Can you summarize what matters most here?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Based on the source material, there are four main areas to understand about TVET instructor capability in Kenya: First, how confident instructors feel about their expertise when teaching core courses. Second, whether instructors have actually received proper training in Whole Youth Development skills—which is about developing young people beyond just technical knowledge. Third, what instructors are actually capable of delivering when it comes to these broader youth development skills. And fourth, what barriers or constraints instructors face when trying to provide soft skills training. Basically, the focus is on whether TVET instructors in Kenya are equipped—both in their confidence and their actual training—to teach not just technical skills, but also the personal and interpersonal skills that young people need."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb7_culture_context", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "tveta_journal_2024.pdf", "source_text": "the instructors’ perception of their expertise in running the core courses in TVET institutions in Kenya. (ii) To determine the extent to which instructors were trained on Whole Youth Development skills in TVET institutions in Kenya. (iii) To assess the instructors’ capabilities within TVET institutions and production of Whole Youth Development skills in Kenya. (iv) To find out instructors’ soft skills training constraints in TVET institutions in Kenya. Methodology This study involved a mixed me"}}
